//Constructor

A constructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is created.

How to identify a constructor?

It has:

Same name as the class
No return type — not even void

Example:

class Student {
public:
    Student() {
        cout << "Constructor called";
    }
};

Here Student() is the constructor because:

Class name = Student
Constructor name = Student
No return type

When we create an object:

Student s1;

The constructor is automatically called.

Main purpose

A constructor is mainly used to initialize an object when it is created.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student() {
        age = 20;
    }
};
Student s1;

cout << s1.age;  // 20

So remember:

Constructor = special function automatically called when an object is created, used mainly for initialization.

Why do we use a constructor?

Without a constructor:

class Student {
public:
    int age;
};

Student s1;
s1.age = 20;

We have to initialize age separately.

With a constructor:

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    Student() {
        age = 20;
    }
};

Student s1;

Now age is automatically initialized when s1 is created.

Main uses of constructors
Initialize data members
Automatically perform setup when an object is created
Avoid writing initialization separately for every object.

Example:

Student s1;
Student s2;
Student s3;

The constructor automatically runs for each object.

So for the basic topic, remember:

We use constructors to automatically initialize/set up an object when it is created.

